ity leaders started a medical school in Dallas to improve the practice of medicine in North Texas. The fledgling school was called the University of Dallas Medical Department, although no such university existed. The school opened its doors Oct. 30, 1900.\r\n\r\nIn 1943, the M.D. Anderson Foundation invited Baylor University College of Medicine to join the newly formed Texas Medical Center. The College opened in Houston July 12, 1943, in a converted Sears, Roebuck & Co. building, with 131 students. Four years later, the College moved into its present site in The Roy and Lillie Cullen Building, the first building completed in the new Texas Medical Center.\r\n\r\nIn 1948, Michael E. DeBakey, M.D., joined the faculty as chair of the Department of Surgery, and the following year, The Graduate School of Biomedical Sciences was established. During the next several years, the College began its affiliation with a number of hospitals, which created superior training facilities for students and residents.\r\n\r\nIn 1969, by mutual agreement, the College separated from Baylor University to become an independent institution. This encouraged broader, nonsectarian support and provided access to federal research funding. The institution\'s name changed to Baylor College of Medicine.\r\n\r\nThat same year, the College entered into an agreement with the state legislature to double its class size in order to increase the number of physicians in Texas. The agreement remains in place today. \r\n\r\n\r\nThe young medical school needed a true affiliation with an established university to survive, and, in 1903, an alliance was formed with Baylor University in Waco. At that time, the name changed to Baylor University College of Medicine. The College struggled in those early years to improve its curriculum, facilities, faculty, and students, and, by 1918, it was the only private medical school in Texas.','',0,'0000-00-00 00:00:00','2011-05-25 20:50:43',NULL,0,'active',NULL,NULL,NULL),(1323,'Baylor Universitty',18,NULL,'a:1:{i:0;s:17:\"Baylor University\";}','Org','','',0,'0000-00-00 00:00:00','2011-05-25 20:50:43',NULL,0,'active',NULL,NULL,NULL),(1324,'Bayou Preservation Association',30,NULL,'a:1:{i:0;s:30:\"Bayou Preservation Association\";}','Org',NULL,'',NULL,'0000-00-00 00:00:00','2011-05-25 20:50:43',NULL,0,'active',NULL,NULL,NULL),(1325,'Berry Consulting',16,NULL,'a:1:{i:0;s:20:\"Berry Consulting LLC\";}','Org',NULL,'',NULL,'0000-00-00 00:00:00','2011-05-25 20:50:43',NULL,0,'active',NULL,NULL,NULL),(1326,'Beth Israel Deaconess Medical Center',36,NULL,'a:1:{i:0;s:33:\"Beth Israel Deaconess Med. Center\";}','Org',NULL,'',NULL,'0000-00-00 00:00:00','2011-05-25 20:50:43',NULL,0,'active',NULL,NULL,NULL),(1327,'Biogen Idec',11,NULL,'a:1:{i:0;s:11:\"Biogen Idec\";}','Org','','',0,'0000-00-00 00:00:00','2011-05-25 20:50:43',NULL,0,'active',NULL,NULL,NULL),(1328,'Biomet Inc.',11,NULL,'a:1:{i:0;s:11:\"Biomet Inc.\";}','Org',NULL,'',NULL,'0000-00-00 00:00:00','2011-05-25 20:50:43',NULL,0,'active',NULL,NULL,NULL),(1329,'Biosense Webster Inc.',21,NULL,'a:1:{i:0;s:21:\"Biosense Webster Inc.\";}','Org',NULL,'',NULL,'0000-00-00 00:00:00','2011-05-25 20:50:43',NULL,0,'active',NULL,NULL,NULL),(1330,'Bioterrorism Consultants Inc.',29,NULL,'a:1:{i:0;s:29:\"Bioterrorism Consultants Inc.\";}','Org',NULL,'',NULL,'0000-00-00 00:00:00','2011-05-25 20:50:43',NULL,0,'active',NULL,NULL,NULL),(1331,'Borough of Wharton (NJ)',23,NULL,'a:1:{i:0;s:29:\"Borough of Wharton Wharton NJ\";}','Org',NULL,'',NULL,'0000-00-00 00:00:00','2011-05-25 20:50:43',NULL,0,'active',NULL,NULL,NULL),(1332,'Boston University School of Public Health',41,NULL,'a:1:{i:0;s:41:\"Boston University School of Public Health\";}','Org',NULL,'',NULL,'0000-00-00 00:00:00','2011-05-25 20:50:43',NULL,0,'active',NULL,NULL,NULL),(1333,'Breast Surgeons of North Texas',30,NULL,'a:1:{i:0;s:30:\"Breast Surgeons of North Texas\";}','Org',NULL,'',NULL,'0000-00-00 00:00:00','2011-05-25 20:50:43',NULL,0,'active',NULL,NULL